---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../../components/nav/SiteHeader.astro';
import TopicList from '../../../components/overview/TopicList.astro';
import {
  DRONE_FAILSAFE_BASE,
  DRONE_FAILSAFE_SOURCE,
  droneFailsafeParts,
} from '../../../components/tutorials/tutorials';
import { GITHUB_URL } from '../../../consts';

const themes = { light: 'github-light', dark: 'github-dark' } as const;
const source = `${GITHUB_URL}/tree/main/${DRONE_FAILSAFE_SOURCE}/part_1_dynamic_causality`;

const run = `cargo run -p dynamic_drone_failsafe --example drone_failsafe_part_1`;

const stages = `process = process
    .bind(|_, state, ctx| model::sense(telemetry, state, ctx))
    .bind(|value, state, ctx| model::detect(value, state, ctx, &detectors))
    .bind(|value, state, ctx| model::decide(value, state, ctx, time_s));`;

const detectors = `pub fn detectors() -> Vec<Detector> {
    vec![
        Causaloid::new(1, gnss_degraded, "satellite positioning degraded"),
        Causaloid::new(2, gnss_no_fix, "satellite positioning without a fix"),
        Causaloid::new(3, link_down, "command link down"),
        Causaloid::new(4, battery_critical, "battery critical"),
    ]
}

fn gnss_degraded(t: Telemetry) -> PropagatingEffect<bool> {
    PropagatingEffect::pure(t.satellites() < DEGRADED_SATELLITES || t.hdop() > DEGRADED_HDOP)
}`;

const collection = `let input = PropagatingEffect::pure(telemetry);
let (outcome, _, _, mut logs) = detectors
    .evaluate_collection(&input, &AggregateLogic::Any, None)
    .into_parts();`;

const ladder = `let (proposed, reason) = if faults.battery_critical {
    (Command::LandNow, "land now: the battery is critical.")
} else if faults.gnss_lost && faults.link_lost {
    (
        Command::LandNow,
        "land now: with no satellite fix and no link, the drone can neither navigate nor receive orders.",
    )
} else if faults.gnss_lost {
    (
        Command::Hold,
        "hold: no satellite fix, but the link is up, so wait for the fix or the operator.",
    )
} else if faults.link_lost {
    (
        Command::ReturnHome,
        "return home: the link is lost, but the fix holds.",
    )
} else {
    (Command::Continue, "continue: no fault.")
};`;

const output = `Dynamic drone fail-safe, part 1: dynamic causality
An inspection drone flies a high-voltage line at night, 40 m above a 30 deg grass slope over a creek.

 time   across   along   agl  sats  hdop  loss   cell  confirmed faults                       fail-safe
   0s      60m      0m   40m    14   0.8    2%  4.05V  none                                   continue
  50s      60m    400m   40m     7   3.5    2%  3.95V  fix degraded                           continue
  57s      56m    440m   40m     3  99.0    2%  3.94V  fix lost                               hold
  69s      32m    440m   40m     3  99.0   95%  3.91V  fix lost, link lost                    land now
  85s       0m    440m   16m     3  99.0   95%  3.25V  fix lost, link lost, battery critical  land now

Touchdown at 91 s, 440 m along the line, 71 m from the nearest person.
It dropped into the creek and was lost.

The controller found every fault in order and took the textbook fail-safe at each step.
It never knew what lay below: the steep grass, the creek, the flat tower pads, the crew.
Part 2 adds that knowledge as context.

Faults and fail-safes from the controller's log:
  t=50 s: Satellite fix degraded: 7 satellites, HDOP 3.5.
  t=57 s: Satellite fix lost: none for 3 s.
  t=57 s: Fail-safe set to hold: no satellite fix, but the link is up, so wait for the fix or the operator.
  t=69 s: Command link lost: 95 % of packets dropped for 5 s.
  t=69 s: Fail-safe set to land now: with no satellite fix and no link, the drone can neither navigate nor receive orders.
  t=85 s: Battery critical: one cell at 3.25 V.`;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Part 1: Dynamic causality — DeepCausality"
  description="Part 1 of the drone fail-safe tutorial: a causal process confirms each fault and applies the textbook fail-safe ladder, and the drone still drops into the creek."
>
  <SiteHeader />
  <article class="static-page">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">Dynamic drone fail-safe · Part 1 of 5</p>
      <h1>Part 1: Dynamic causality</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        One causal process runs the controller, three stages each second. It confirms every fault
        in order and applies the textbook fail-safe at each step. The drone still drops into the
        creek, because the controller knows nothing about the ground below it.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Run it</h2>
      <Code code={run} lang="bash" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <p>
        The source lives in <a href={source}><code>part_1_dynamic_causality</code></a>.
      </p>

      <h2>One causal process, three stages</h2>
      <p>
        Each second, the controller binds three stages into its causal process:
      </p>
      <Code code={stages} lang="rust" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <ol>
        <li><code>sense</code> takes the telemetry into the process and logs it.</li>
        <li><code>detect</code> runs the fault detectors and confirms the faults that persist.</li>
        <li><code>decide</code> applies the fail-safe ladder.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Each stage receives the previous stage's value, the process state and the context, and
        returns a new value, state and context with its own log entries. An error in any stage stops the chain. The
        state carries memory from one second to the next: how long GPS and the command link to the pilot
        have been gone, the confirmed faults and the latched fail-safe. In part 1 the process has no context,
        so the controller knows only what the drone reports about itself.
      </p>

      <h2>Four detectors, one collection</h2>
      <p>
        Each detector is a causaloid: a threshold on one second's telemetry.
      </p>
      <Code code={detectors} lang="rust" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <p>
        <code>detect</code> evaluates the four as a collection. With <code>AggregateLogic::Any</code>, the collection says whether anything is wrong; only then does each detector say what.
      </p>
      <Code code={collection} lang="rust" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <p>
        The process state confirms lost GPS after 3 s and a lost link after 5 s, so a dropped
        packet does not trigger a fail-safe.
      </p>

      <h2>The textbook ladder</h2>
      <p>
        <code>decide</code> applies the standard fail-safe ladder. A critical battery, or lost
        GPS with a lost link, lands the drone where it is. Lost GPS alone holds it for the
        operator, and a lost link alone flies it home.
      </p>
      <Code code={ladder} lang="rust" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <p>
        Once an emergency begins, the fail-safe never steps back down in flight: <code>decide</code> replaces the latched command only with a more severe one.
      </p>

      <h2>What happened</h2>
      <Code code={output} lang="text" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />
      <p>
        The controller confirmed every fault in order and took the textbook fail-safe each time.
        While it held and then descended, the night wind carried the drone 72 m down the slope and
        into the creek. The controller never knew what lay below it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href={`${DRONE_FAILSAFE_BASE}dynamic-context/`}>Part 2</a> gives it that knowledge as
        context.
      </p>

      <TopicList label="Dynamic drone fail-safe" current="dynamic-causality" items={droneFailsafeParts} base={DRONE_FAILSAFE_BASE} />
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
